We examine the uniqueness of solutions for the given evolution equation:

\begin{equation} \label{eq1}
\begin{cases}
\displaystyle u_t -\big(J_{\gamma}(u) \cdot \nabla u\big)_t +
\nabla \cdot \big(J_{\gamma}(u)\big) = 0, \quad
\text{on } (0, \infty) \times \mathbb{R}^d
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

with specific initial conditions, where the domain is $\mathbb{R}^d$, and the initial data, depending on the parameter $\gamma$, can be either Lipschitz continuous or discontinuous. The uniqueness of the solution to the non-linear equation depends heavily on the initial data, and we show that the solution to the non-linear equation remains regular if the initial data are regular, but may become discontinuous if the initial data do. Furthermore, the solution to the non-linear equation is shown to be well-posed in the sense of Hadamard, and the solution to the non-linear equation is also shown to be continuously dependent on the initial data.
